{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part2.json\\\")); print(json.dumps(p[\\\"answers\\\"],ensure_ascii=False,separators=(\\\",\\\",\\\":\\\")))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"answer\":\"B11\",\"article\":3,\"statements_with_figures\":\"none kept\"},{\"answer\":\"B12\",\"article\":4,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"Wall Street consensus, as reported by Motley Fool, expects Nvidia to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year (the article later ties 'next year's earnings' to fiscal 2028, which ends in January 2028).\",\"passages\":[\"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Wall Street consensus\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"period\":\"this year\",\"value\":\"91%\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Wall Street consensus\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"period\":\"next year\",\"value\":\"66%\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Wall Street consensus\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia is expected by consensus to report earnings per share of $15.68 in fiscal 2028, which the article says ends in January 2028.\",\"passages\":[\"For fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year, Nvidia is expected to have earnings per share of $15.68.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Wall Street consensus\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"earnings per share\",\"period\":\"fiscal 2028, which ends in January of that year\",\"value\":\"$15.68\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Wall Street consensus\"}],\"links\":\"none\"}]},{\"answer\":\"B13\",\"article\":6,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier, of which $89 billion — roughly 92.5% of total revenue — came from the data center segment. Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126%, and earnings per share were $2.46, up 128% from a year earlier; operating expenses were $8.4 billion for the quarter and gross margin was 75%.\",\"passages\":[\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"],\"statement_party\":\"NVIDIA\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Revenue\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company total; up 106% from a year ago\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"Data center segment revenue\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"$89 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"Net income\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"$59.68 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Up 126% from a year ago\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":4,\"measure\":\"Earnings per share\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"$2.46\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Up 128% from last year\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":5,\"measure\":\"Operating expenses\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"$8.4 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"For the quarter\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":6,\"measure\":\"Gross margin\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\"value\":\"75%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e5\",\"summary\":\"The consensus revenue estimate for NVIDIA's next quarterly report, expected in late November, is $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the same quarter a year earlier.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year.\"],\"statement_party\":null,\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Revenue consensus estimate\",\"period\":\"Next quarter, likely reported in late November\",\"value\":\"$109.0 billion\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"Consensus; up 91.2% from the previous year\",\"party\":null}],\"links\":\"none\"}]},{\"answer\":\"B14\",\"article\":3,\"statements_with_figures\":\"none kept\"},{\"answer\":\"B15\",\"article\":8,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"ev-1\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia's fiscal 2026, ended January 2026, produced revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion, and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, versus a 55.8% net margin in the prior fiscal year, according to fool.com's 25 September 2026 comparison article.\",\"passages\":[\"Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Revenue\",\"period\":\"Fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\"value\":\"$215.9 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company total, approximately, as reported by the article\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"Revenue growth year over year\",\"period\":\"Fiscal 2026\",\"value\":\"65.5%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Approximately, versus prior fiscal year\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"Net income\",\"period\":\"Fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\"value\":\"$120.1 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company total, approximately\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":4,\"measure\":\"Net margin\",\"period\":\"Fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\"value\":\"55.6%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Approximately; prior fiscal year was 55.8%\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}]},{\"id\":\"ev-2\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia's data center business generated $89 billion in its latest reported quarter, with total revenue also more than doubling year over year, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion in revenue while assuming no data center compute sales to China at all, per fool.com's 25 September 2026 article.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Data center revenue\",\"period\":\"Latest reported quarter\",\"value\":\"$89 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Data center business segment only\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"Revenue guidance\",\"period\":\"Next quarter\",\"value\":\"$108 billion\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":\"Company outlook; excludes any China compute sales\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}]}]},{\"answer\":\"B16\",\"article\":1,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"NVIDIA's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in overall revenue, with roughly 92.5% of sales coming from the AI infrastructure businesses, as reported by GuruFocus. The item does not name the quarter or fiscal year.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"],\"statement_party\":\"NVIDIA\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"data-center revenue\",\"period\":\"latest quarter\",\"value\":\"$89 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"data-center / infrastructure businesses segment\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"overall revenue\",\"period\":\"latest quarter\",\"value\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company total\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"share of sales from infrastructure businesses\",\"period\":\"latest quarter\",\"value\":\"roughly 92.5%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"author's calculation from the stated data-center and total revenue figures; described as 'roughly'\",\"party\":\"GuruFocus\"}],\"links\":\"none\"}]},{\"answer\":\"B17\",\"article\":3,\"statements_with_figures\":\"none kept\"},{\"answer\":\"B18\",\"article\":5,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia reported revenue of $96.2 billion for its fiscal second quarter, 106% higher than the same period a year earlier, with a 75% gross margin.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"106%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"year-over-year\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"gross margin\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"75%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia guided to $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\",\"passages\":[\"Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue guidance\",\"period\":\"current quarter\",\"value\":\"$108 billion\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":\"company guidance\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"sequential revenue growth\",\"period\":\"current quarter\",\"value\":\"17%\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":\"sequential, derived from company guidance\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e3\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\",\"passages\":[\"The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue growth guidance\",\"period\":\"next fiscal year\",\"value\":\"roughly 70%\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":\"company guidance\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e4\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July; the article calculates days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months.\",\"A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\",\"Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"total assets growth\",\"period\":\"past six months\",\"value\":\"more than 50%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"receivables\",\"period\":\"January\",\"value\":\"$38.5 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"receivables\",\"period\":\"end of July\",\"value\":\"$63.1 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":4,\"measure\":\"days sales outstanding\",\"period\":\"one quarter\",\"value\":\"45 to 60 days\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company-wide; calculated by article\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-1\",\"new_question\":false,\"reason\":\"Shows the balance-sheet scale of customer financing: receivables and days sales outstanding rose as Nvidia extended payment terms, informing the total credit-support question; these are not guarantee amounts.\"}]},{\"id\":\"e6\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\",\"passages\":[\"For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"data center lease guarantee\",\"period\":null,\"value\":\"$105 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"up to; for OpenAI data center lease obligations\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-1\",\"new_question\":false,\"reason\":\"Directly informs the thesis's tracked amount of credit support and guarantees to AI labs/cloud partners: Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of OpenAI data center lease obligations.\"}]}]},{\"answer\":\"B19\",\"article\":6,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"NVIDIA's second quarter of fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier, of which $89 billion — roughly 92.5% — came from the data center segment. Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126%, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Operating expenses totaled $8.4 billion for the quarter and gross margin was 75%, as reported in the article's account of the company's most recent earnings report.\",\"passages\":[\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"],\"statement_party\":\"NVIDIA\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Revenue\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company total; up 106% from a year ago\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"Data center revenue\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"$89 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Data center segment; roughly 92.5% of total revenue\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"Net income\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"$59.68 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company total; up 126% from a year ago\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":4,\"measure\":\"Earnings per share\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"$2.46\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Up 128% from last year\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":5,\"measure\":\"Operating expenses\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"$8.4 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Total operating expenses for the quarter\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"},{\"n\":6,\"measure\":\"Gross margin\",\"period\":\"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026)\",\"value\":\"75%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"Company gross margin for the quarter\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"summary\":\"CEO Jensen Huang said the parts of NVIDIA's market outside large system builders — sovereign AI, regional AIs, neoclouds, AI start-ups and enterprises — represent about half of the company's business and are growing 100% a year, while most companies lack the skills or desire to build an entire full-stack AI factory platform themselves.\",\"passages\":[\"\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\"],\"statement_party\":\"NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang)\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Growth rate of sovereign AI, regional AI, neocloud, AI start-up and enterprise business (about half of total business)\",\"period\":\"Current, as stated alongside Q2 fiscal 2027 results\",\"value\":\"100% a year\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"CEO Jensen Huang's description of the roughly half of NVIDIA's business outside large full-stack builders\",\"party\":\"NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang)\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e6\",\"summary\":\"The consensus revenue estimate for NVIDIA's next quarterly report, likely in late November (fiscal 2027 third quarter), is $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year, with more of the same expected for the following year after the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Consensus of analysts (reported by fool.com)\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"Revenue (consensus estimate)\",\"period\":\"Next quarter, likely reported in late November 2026\",\"value\":\"$109.0 billion\",\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"Analyst consensus; up 91.2% from the previous year\",\"party\":\"Consensus of analysts (reported by fool.com)\"}],\"links\":\"none\"}]},{\"answer\":\"B20\",\"article\":5,\"statements_with_figures\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin, according to the article's account of the company's recently reported quarter.\",\"passages\":[\"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year.\",\"The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":\"recently\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company total revenue\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"year-over-year revenue growth\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"106%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"versus same period last year\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"gross margin\",\"period\":\"fiscal second quarter\",\"value\":\"75%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Nvidia\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia's management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, the article reports.\",\"passages\":[\"The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":\"recently\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"period\":\"next fiscal year\",\"value\":\"roughly 70%\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Nvidia management\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e3\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for its current quarter, which the article says represents 17% sequential growth.\",\"passages\":[\"Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"period\":\"current quarter\",\"value\":\"$108 billion\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Nvidia management\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"sequential revenue growth\",\"period\":\"current quarter\",\"value\":\"17%\",\"kind\":\"guidance\",\"basis\":\"sequential, versus the reported fiscal second quarter\",\"party\":\"fool.com (article author's calculation from the guidance)\"}],\"links\":\"none\"},{\"id\":\"e4\",\"summary\":\"Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, driven in large part by receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July; the article calculates that days sales outstanding rose from 45 to 60 days in one quarter, meaning customers are taking longer to pay.\",\"passages\":[\"However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months.\",\"As mentioned earlier, Nvidia's total assets grew rapidly. A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\",\"Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\"],\"statement_party\":\"Nvidia\",\"statement_date\":null,\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"measure\":\"total assets growth\",\"period\":\"over the past six months\",\"value\":\"more than 50%\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"company balance sheet\",\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":2,\"measure\":\"receivables\",\"period\":\"January\",\"value\":\"$38.5 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":3,\"measure\":\"receivables\",\"period\":\"end of July\",\"value\":\"$63.1 billion\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":null,\"party\":\"Nvidia\"},{\"n\":4,\"measure\":\"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\"period\":\"one quarter\",\"value\":\"45 to 60 days\",\"kind\":\"actual\",\"basis\":\"average collection period calculated from reported figures\",\"party\":\"fool.com (article author's calculation)\"}],\"links\":[{\"question\":\"clean-1\",\"new_question\":false,\"reason\":\"Receivables growth and the rise in days sales outstanding are the working-capital counterpart of Nvidia helping finance customers' orders; the article links part of it to extended payment terms (separate item). It is not itself a guarantee or a credit-support commitment counted toward the thesis's disclosed-amount outcome, but it informs how the support is showing up on the balance sheet.\"}]}]}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part2.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([a for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (6,8)],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/reader-packet-1-part2.json\\\")); print(json.dumps([a for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (6,8)],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"article\": 6, \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\", \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\", \"named_figures\": [{\"figure\": 1, \"text\": \"the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion for the next report\"}, {\"figure\": 2, \"text\": \"revenue of $96.2 billion in the second quarter of fiscal 2027\"}], \"open_questions\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]}, {\"article\": 8, \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\", \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\", \"named_figures\": [], \"open_questions\": null}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answer\":\"B11\",\"statements\":[],\"named\":[],\"note\":\"\"}\n{\"answer\":\"B12\",\"statements\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":2,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e2\",\"statement_party\":\"wrong\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"wrong\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null}],\"named\":[{\"figure\":1,\"statement\":\"e2\",\"n\":1}],\"note\":\"The article does not say who made the fiscal 2028 EPS forecast.\"}\n{\"answer\":\"B13\",\"statements\":[{\"id\":\"e1\",\"statement_party\":\"right\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":2,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":3,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":4,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":5,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"},{\"n\":6,\"party\":\"right\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null},{\"id\":\"e5\",\"statement_party\":\"missing\",\"statement_date\":\"unknown_ok\",\"figures\":[{\"n\":1,\"party\":\"missing\",\"measure\":\"right\",\"value\":\"right\",\"kind\":\"right\"}],\"links\":[],\"should_link\":null}],\"named\":[{\"figure\":1,\"statement\":\"e5\",\"n\":1},{\"figure\":2,\"statement\":\"e1\",\"n\":1}],\"note\":\"The $109 billion forecast is identified as a consensus estimate, but its source is omitted.\"}\n{\"answer\":\